{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VXgjRWMPmE9S8Y4EkpuXQD","type":"tool_result","content":"func (l *lane) run(ctx context.Context, s snap) (Result, error) {\n\tif !l.persistent {\n\t\tif err := l.addWorktree(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn Result{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tdefer func() { _ = l.g.WorktreeRemove(context.WithoutCancel(ctx), l.cfg.RepoDir, l.wt) }()\n\t}\n\tdeclared := s.declared\n\n\tvar base, prev string\n\tfor attempt := 1; ; attempt++ {\n\t\tvar err error\n\t\tif base, err = l.fetch(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, prev)\n\t\t\treturn Result{Attempts: attempt - 1}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tfrom := prev\n\t\tif attempt == 1 {\n\t\t\tfrom = s.base\n\t\t}\n\t\tif from != \"\" {\n\t\t\tchanged, err := l.g.ChangedPaths(ctx, l.wt, from, base, declared...)\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\t\t\treturn Result{Attempts: attempt - 1}, err\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif len(changed) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\t\t\treturn Result{Attempts: attempt - 1}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s\", ErrConflict, strings.Join(changed, \", \"))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tres, retry, err := l.attempt(ctx, s, base, false)\n\t\tres.Attempts = attempt\n\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\tif res.NoOp || res.Synthetic {\n\t\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn res, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\tif !retry || attempt >= maxPushes {\n\t\t\treturn res, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tl.log.DebugContext(ctx, \"committer.retry\", \"branch\", l.branch, \"attempt\", attempt, \"err\", err.Error())\n\t\tprev = base\n\t\tselect {\n\t\tcase <-time.After(l.backoff(attempt)):\n\t\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\t\treturn res, ctx.Err()\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// attempt applies the snapshot on base, commits and pushes. retry reports\n// whether a failure is worth another fetch-and-apply round. With create, base\n// is the commit a new branch is cut from: nothing staged is ErrInvalid (a\n// branch with no commit cannot become a PR) and the push's lease expects the\n// ref to be absent, so a branch that exists is ErrBranchExists, never retried.\nfunc (l *lane) attempt(ctx context.Context, s snap, base string, create bool) (res Result, retry bool, err error) {\n\tif err := l.g.ResetHard(ctx, l.wt, base); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tif err := l.g.CleanAll(ctx, l.wt); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tdeclared, msg := s.declared, s.msg\n\tvar writes, deletes, execs []string\n\tfor _, f := range s.files {\n\t\tfull := filepath.Join(l.wt, filepath.FromSlash(f.path))\n\t\t// Defence in depth behind CheckCodePath/CheckPath: never touch a\n\t\t// path that resolves outside the worktree.\n\t\tif rel, err := filepath.Rel(l.wt, full); err != nil || rel == \"..\" || strings.HasPrefix(rel, \"../\") {\n\t\t\tge := &GuardError{}\n\t\t\tge.add(f.path, \"escapes the worktree\")\n\t\t\treturn res, false, ge\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := checkParents(l.wt, f.path); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif f.del {\n\t\t\tif err := os.Remove(full); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n\t\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"remove %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdeletes = append(deletes, f.path)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(full), 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"mkdir for %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tdata := f.data\n\t\tif f.merge != nil {\n\t\t\tcur, rerr := os.ReadFile(full)\n\t\t\tif rerr != nil && !errors.Is(rerr, os.ErrNotExist) {\n\t\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"read %s: %w\", f.path, rerr)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tmerged, merr := f.merge(cur, rerr == nil, f.data)\n\t\t\tif merr != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s: %v\", ErrMerge, f.path, merr)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdata = merged\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(full, data, 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"write %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\t// WriteFile keeps the mode of an existing file, so set it explicitly.\n\t\tif err := os.Chmod(full, fileMode(f.exec)); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"chmod %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\twrites = append(writes, f.path)\n\t\tif f.exec {\n\t\t\texecs = append(execs, f.path)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(writes) > 0 {\n\t\tif err := l.g.AddForce(ctx, l.wt, writes...); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(deletes) > 0 {\n\t\tif err := l.g.RmCached(ctx, l.wt, deletes...); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(execs) > 0 {\n\t\tif err := l.g.SetExec(ctx, l.wt, execs...); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tchanges, err := l.g.StagedChanges(ctx, l.wt)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tif err := CheckStaged(declared, changes); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tif err := checkModes(s, changes); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tnothing := func() (Result, bool, error) {\n\t\tif create {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: nothing to commit on a new branch\", ErrInvalid)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn Result{SHA: base, NoOp: true}, false, nil\n\t}\n\tif len(changes) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nothing()\n\t}\n\tcr, err := l.fx.Commit(ctx, effects.CommitOp{Dir: l.wt, Message: msg, Author: l.author})\n\tif errors.Is(err, effects.ErrNothingToCommit) {\n\t\treturn nothing()\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tlease := base\n\tif create {\n\t\tlease = zeroSHA\n\t}\n\terr = l.fx.Push(ctx, effects.PushOp{Dir: l.wt, Remote: l.cfg.RemoteURL, Ref: \"HEAD:refs/heads/\" + l.branch,\n\t\tForceWithLease: lease, Token: l.cfg.Token})\n\tif create && (errors.Is(err, effects.ErrNonFastForward) || errors.Is(err, effects.ErrStaleLease)) {\n\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %v\", ErrBranchExists, err)\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, errors.Is(err, effects.ErrNonFastForward) || errors.Is(err, effects.ErrStaleLease), err\n\t}\n\treturn Result{SHA: cr.SHA, Synthetic: cr.Synthetic}, false, nil\n}\n\n// fetch updates the private upstream ref and returns its SHA.\nTODO.md\nlayout.md\nproposal.md\nrunbooks\n129:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n158:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n160:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init`'s: it commits only `.ard/config.yaml` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n325:6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n332:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up that will use the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound`); until then a conflict, like a red required check, goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n378:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n450:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n486:| `.ard/<issue#>/` written on `main` and on the branch | Conflicts when the PR merges | Ownership moves to the branch when it is cut |\n490:| Parallel triage roles share `plan.md` | Lost sections, conflicting commits | One triage job per issue at a time |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a5c288b-fd98-412c-9a5a-a71521c6ec72","uuid":"433e1f6a-2c5e-4786-8c1e-d6df9fca5560","timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:29:24.921Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"func (l *lane) run(ctx context.Context, s snap) (Result, error) {\n\tif !l.persistent {\n\t\tif err := l.addWorktree(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn Result{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tdefer func() { _ = l.g.WorktreeRemove(context.WithoutCancel(ctx), l.cfg.RepoDir, l.wt) }()\n\t}\n\tdeclared := s.declared\n\n\tvar base, prev string\n\tfor attempt := 1; ; attempt++ {\n\t\tvar err error\n\t\tif base, err = l.fetch(ctx); err != nil {\n\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, prev)\n\t\t\treturn Result{Attempts: attempt - 1}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tfrom := prev\n\t\tif attempt == 1 {\n\t\t\tfrom = s.base\n\t\t}\n\t\tif from != \"\" {\n\t\t\tchanged, err := l.g.ChangedPaths(ctx, l.wt, from, base, declared...)\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\t\t\treturn Result{Attempts: attempt - 1}, err\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif len(changed) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\t\t\treturn Result{Attempts: attempt - 1}, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s\", ErrConflict, strings.Join(changed, \", \"))\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tres, retry, err := l.attempt(ctx, s, base, false)\n\t\tres.Attempts = attempt\n\t\tif err == nil {\n\t\t\tif res.NoOp || res.Synthetic {\n\t\t\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\treturn res, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\tl.reset(ctx, base)\n\t\tif !retry || attempt >= maxPushes {\n\t\t\treturn res, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tl.log.DebugContext(ctx, \"committer.retry\", \"branch\", l.branch, \"attempt\", attempt, \"err\", err.Error())\n\t\tprev = base\n\t\tselect {\n\t\tcase <-time.After(l.backoff(attempt)):\n\t\tcase <-ctx.Done():\n\t\t\treturn res, ctx.Err()\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// attempt applies the snapshot on base, commits and pushes. retry reports\n// whether a failure is worth another fetch-and-apply round. With create, base\n// is the commit a new branch is cut from: nothing staged is ErrInvalid (a\n// branch with no commit cannot become a PR) and the push's lease expects the\n// ref to be absent, so a branch that exists is ErrBranchExists, never retried.\nfunc (l *lane) attempt(ctx context.Context, s snap, base string, create bool) (res Result, retry bool, err error) {\n\tif err := l.g.ResetHard(ctx, l.wt, base); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tif err := l.g.CleanAll(ctx, l.wt); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tdeclared, msg := s.declared, s.msg\n\tvar writes, deletes, execs []string\n\tfor _, f := range s.files {\n\t\tfull := filepath.Join(l.wt, filepath.FromSlash(f.path))\n\t\t// Defence in depth behind CheckCodePath/CheckPath: never touch a\n\t\t// path that resolves outside the worktree.\n\t\tif rel, err := filepath.Rel(l.wt, full); err != nil || rel == \"..\" || strings.HasPrefix(rel, \"../\") {\n\t\t\tge := &GuardError{}\n\t\t\tge.add(f.path, \"escapes the worktree\")\n\t\t\treturn res, false, ge\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := checkParents(l.wt, f.path); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tif f.del {\n\t\t\tif err := os.Remove(full); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) {\n\t\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"remove %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdeletes = append(deletes, f.path)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(full), 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"mkdir for %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tdata := f.data\n\t\tif f.merge != nil {\n\t\t\tcur, rerr := os.ReadFile(full)\n\t\t\tif rerr != nil && !errors.Is(rerr, os.ErrNotExist) {\n\t\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"read %s: %w\", f.path, rerr)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tmerged, merr := f.merge(cur, rerr == nil, f.data)\n\t\t\tif merr != nil {\n\t\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %s: %v\", ErrMerge, f.path, merr)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tdata = merged\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(full, data, 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"write %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\t// WriteFile keeps the mode of an existing file, so set it explicitly.\n\t\tif err := os.Chmod(full, fileMode(f.exec)); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"chmod %s: %w\", f.path, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\twrites = append(writes, f.path)\n\t\tif f.exec {\n\t\t\texecs = append(execs, f.path)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(writes) > 0 {\n\t\tif err := l.g.AddForce(ctx, l.wt, writes...); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(deletes) > 0 {\n\t\tif err := l.g.RmCached(ctx, l.wt, deletes...); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(execs) > 0 {\n\t\tif err := l.g.SetExec(ctx, l.wt, execs...); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tchanges, err := l.g.StagedChanges(ctx, l.wt)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tif err := CheckStaged(declared, changes); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tif err := checkModes(s, changes); err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tnothing := func() (Result, bool, error) {\n\t\tif create {\n\t\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: nothing to commit on a new branch\", ErrInvalid)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn Result{SHA: base, NoOp: true}, false, nil\n\t}\n\tif len(changes) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nothing()\n\t}\n\tcr, err := l.fx.Commit(ctx, effects.CommitOp{Dir: l.wt, Message: msg, Author: l.author})\n\tif errors.Is(err, effects.ErrNothingToCommit) {\n\t\treturn nothing()\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, false, err\n\t}\n\tlease := base\n\tif create {\n\t\tlease = zeroSHA\n\t}\n\terr = l.fx.Push(ctx, effects.PushOp{Dir: l.wt, Remote: l.cfg.RemoteURL, Ref: \"HEAD:refs/heads/\" + l.branch,\n\t\tForceWithLease: lease, Token: l.cfg.Token})\n\tif create && (errors.Is(err, effects.ErrNonFastForward) || errors.Is(err, effects.ErrStaleLease)) {\n\t\treturn res, false, fmt.Errorf(\"%w: %v\", ErrBranchExists, err)\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn res, errors.Is(err, effects.ErrNonFastForward) || errors.Is(err, effects.ErrStaleLease), err\n\t}\n\treturn Result{SHA: cr.SHA, Synthetic: cr.Synthetic}, false, nil\n}\n\n// fetch updates the private upstream ref and returns its SHA.\nTODO.md\nlayout.md\nproposal.md\nrunbooks\n129:Close-out checks `plan.md` against the job's base commit, but the committer writes it onto the latest `main`. So at commit time the committer replaces only the job's own `##` section in the current `main` copy (adding it at the end if absent, dropping it if the job removed it) and keeps every other section as `main` has it; a section another role committed after the job's base is not reverted. This runs again on each push retry. If the `main` copy cannot be merged (the job's heading appears twice, or its section holds a heading-like line), the job fails closed as `needs-human` with a `plan.md conflict` reason; like an allowlist denial, only the redacted log and the job's usage record are committed, never `plan.md` or the job's other files.\n158:**One writer location per issue directory at a time.** Until the worker branch exists, `main` owns `.ard/<issue#>/`; scanners and triage write there. Once the branch is cut, the branch owns it; workers and reviewers commit to the PR, and a scanner with new findings comments on the issue instead. This keeps the PR merge free of conflicts in `.ard/`.\n160:**One committer for main.** Close-outs never push. They hand a file set to the controller's committer, which keeps its own clean worktree of `main`, commits as `ard[bot]` with `ard(<stage>:<role>): #<issue> <summary> [skip ci]`, re-applies its snapshot on the fetched head, pushes with a lease, and retries up to 3 times (re-applying the whole-file snapshot is the rebase; if upstream changed one of its paths the request fails with a conflict instead of overwriting). A replay of an unchanged tree is a no-op that returns the upstream head. It refuses any change outside the `.ard/<issue#>/` directories. Worker code takes a separate lane: `Branches.CommitCode` commits only paths `committer.CheckCodePath` accepts (nothing under `.ard/`, `.ard-input/` or `.github/`, no `.git` or secret-shaped names), only to `worker/` branches, creating a branch with a push whose lease expects it to be absent and refusing to overwrite a path that changed on the branch since the job started. The one other lane is `ard init`'s: it commits only `.ard/config.yaml` and files under the four role trees, only to the new branch `ard-init` cut from `main`, with a push whose lease expects the ref to be absent, so it creates the branch and never updates an existing one, and opens a PR; a human merges it with a squash or merge commit, so the bot-commit check never sees a bot commit outside `.ard/<issue#>/` on main's first-parent history. Because the bot must bypass branch protection on `main`, a detective check audits it: the workflow `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` walks the first-parent history of `main` (the last 500 commits, or everything after the repository variable `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`) and fails the run, listing the commits and paths in the job summary, when a commit made by the bot changes anything but regular files under a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. Bot commits are identified by **committer** (the App's noreply email or login), not author, because a squash merge of a bot PR has the bot as author. If the 500-commit window is full and its oldest commit is the bot's, the check fails closed until `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE` is set, so a bad commit cannot be flushed out of view. It runs hourly as well as on push, because `[skip ci]` suppresses the push trigger. It fails closed when the `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` repository variable is unset. It reads both variables from repository settings and lives in `.github/workflows/`, which the App cannot write. Alerting is the failed run only; it does not open issues or pause ard.\n325:6. **Two commits per round.** The loop commits the code through the committer's code lane (`Branches.CommitCode`) with the title `[<type>: ]<issue title> (#<issue>)`, the redacted summary (secrets and host paths) as the body and an `Ard-Job: <job-id>` trailer, no `[skip ci]`. `<type>` comes from the issue's `type:*` label (`feature` is `feat`, `bug` is `fix`; `docs`, `chore`, `refactor` and `test` keep their names) and with no `type:*` label the `<type>: ` part is dropped and the title is `<issue title> (#<issue>)`. The prefix is never taken from the plan or the lead's routing, so it depends on a maintainer applying `type:*` at intake. At the end close-out commits `worker-<role>.md` and the redacted logs of every agent step to the same branch (or to `main`, as triage's are, when the job stopped before its first push). `.ard/<issue#>/` never goes through the code lane. A push failure (branch exists, conflict, branch gone, guard) fails the job with no automatic retry.\n332:**Review feedback loops back here.** The `reviewfix` loop (#41) starts a round when every requested reviewer has finished and at least one ended `ard:review:<role>:changes`, backed by that role's close-out record (a label alone does not count). It records a `review_round` event in `_controller.db` (counted when requested, ordered by event ID, local to that database) and removes `ard:worker:<role>:done` from the issue, so the worker loop claims it again. The worker fixes on the same branch and reads `review-feedback.md`. When the fix lands with code changes, every requested reviewer runs again at the new head (their `:done` and `:changes` labels come off), because merge-gating approvals must cover the merged head. A round that changes no code, or one that would pass `max_review_rounds` (default 3), posts one comment and adds `ard:needs-human` on the issue; it is not repeated on the same head after a person removes the label. Removing `ard:needs-human` and pushing a new head caps again at once and never reruns reviewers; to continue, raise max_review_rounds, or fix the PR and remove the ard:review:<role>:changes labels so reviewers rerun. Merger conflict and rebase rounds are a follow-up that will use the same round API (`reviewfix.RequestRound`); until then a conflict, like a red required check, goes to `ard:needs-human`.\n378:- GitHub has computed that the branch does not conflict with `main`. Only a computed `mergeable: false` or `mergeable_state: dirty` is a conflict: it gets `ard:needs-human` and one comment, and a follow-up to #41 will turn this into a rebase round (`reviewfix.RequestRound`) that counts toward `max_review_rounds`. A `mergeable` that is still null or unknown, and a 405 \"not mergeable\" from the merge call, mean GitHub is still computing: the merger retries next tick and never labels\n450:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n486:| `.ard/<issue#>/` written on `main` and on the branch | Conflicts when the PR merges | Ownership moves to the branch when it is cut |\n490:| Parallel triage roles share `plan.md` | Lost sections, conflicting commits | One triage job per issue at a time |","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VXgjRWMPmE9S8Y4EkpuXQD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
